Thanks — I can help. Quick first step: there’s an inconsistency in the numbers you gave that we should fix before building CAC/LTV and the finance narrative.

- You said cash = $5.2M and runway = 14 months. That implies monthly net burn ≈ 5,200,000 / 14 ≈ $371,429/mo.
- But you also said burn = $45K/month. If true, runway = 5,200,000 / 45,000 ≈ 115.6 months (≈ 9.6 years).

Which of those is correct? Is $45K a different line item (e.g., incremental cash burn after some revenue), or is the 14 months runway the figure that includes planned spend or hires? I’ll proceed once you confirm.

What I need from you to compute CAC and LTV precisely
- Time period to use (last quarter, last 12 months, trailing 12 months)
- Sales & Marketing spend for that period (total S&M = marketing + sales)
- Number of new customers acquired during that same period
- Total active customers (or customers at period start and end)
- Revenue period and whether revenue is subscription/recurring or one-time (you gave $3.2M last quarter — good)
- Gross margin (or cost of goods sold %); if you don’t have it, tell me product vs service mix
- Churn rate (monthly or annual) or average customer lifetime (months or years)
If you give those, I’ll compute exact CAC, ARPU, LTV, LTV:CAC, and payback period.

If you want to move forward now, below are (a) the formulas I’ll use and (b) example scenarios so you can see how the narrative will look.

Core formulas (what I’ll calculate)
- CAC = Total Sales & Marketing Spend / Number of New Customers (over the same period)
- ARPU (monthly) = Revenue for period / Average customers during period / number of months in period
  or ARPU (period) = Revenue / Customers
- Monthly churn = Customers lost during month / Customers at start of month
- Average customer lifetime (months) = 1 / monthly_churn (for constant churn, subscription model)
- LTV = ARPU_monthly × Gross Margin % × Average customer lifetime (months)
  Equivalent: LTV = (ARPU_monthly × gross_margin) / monthly_churn
- LTV:CAC ratio = LTV / CAC
- CAC payback (months) = CAC / (ARPU_monthly × gross_margin)

Benchmarks and guidance to use in the finance narrative
- Healthy target LTV:CAC: ≥ 3 (classic rule of thumb). Lower acceptable for high-growth companies if payback is quick and you have scale funding.
- CAC payback target: < 12 months for SaaS (shorter is better). For enterprise B2B, 12–24 months can be OK if LTV is very high.
- Improve LTV by increasing ARPU (upsells, pricing), improving gross margin, and reducing churn.
- Improve CAC by increasing conversion efficiency, cheaper channels, better product virality, and sales efficiency.

Example scenarios using your revenue ($3.2M last quarter)
Assume quarterly revenue = $3.2M → monthly revenue ≈ $1.0667M.

Scenario A — mid-market SaaS (illustrative)
- Avg customers = 400
- ARPU_monthly = (3,200,000 / 3) / 400 ≈ $2,667
- Gross margin = 70%
- Monthly churn = 2% → Avg lifetime = 1 / 0.02 = 50 months
- Suppose S&M last quarter = $400,000 and new customers last quarter = 100 → CAC = 400,000 / 100 = $4,000
Calculations:
- LTV = 2,667 × 0.70 × 50 ≈ $93,345
- LTV:CAC ≈ 93,345 / 4,000 ≈ 23.3 (very strong)
- CAC payback = 4,000 / (2,667 × 0.70) ≈ 2.1 months (excellent)

Scenario B — higher-volume, lower ARPU (illustrative)
- Avg customers = 4,000
- ARPU_monthly = (3,200,000 / 3) / 4,000 ≈ $267
- Gross margin = 60%
- Monthly churn = 4% → Avg lifetime = 25 months
- Suppose S&M last quarter = $800,000 and new customers = 1,000 → CAC = $800
Calculations:
- LTV = 267 × 0.60 × 25 ≈ $4,005
- LTV:CAC ≈ 4,005 / 800 ≈ 5.0 (healthy)
- CAC payback = 800 / (267 × 0.60) ≈ 5.0 months (good)

How I’ll shape the finance narrative once numbers are confirmed
- Start: current cash, runway (show calculation + explain any reconciling items — capex, hiring plans, one-time items)
- Unit economics section: CAC, ARPU, gross margin, churn, LTV, LTV:CAC, payback period — each with the formula and the current value
- Sensitivity analysis: show 3 scenarios (base, upside, downside) that vary CAC and churn and show impact on LTV and payback
- Actionable levers: pricing changes, product-led growth, channel optimization, retention programs, and cost control — each with estimated impact on runway and valuation metrics
- One-slide summary with 3 KPIs to watch monthly and recommended targets

Next steps — pick one:
1) Confirm which runway/burn figure is correct and provide the inputs I listed (S&M spend, new customers, churn, gross margin, total customers). I’ll compute exact CAC/LTV and produce a short slide-style narrative and sensitivity table.
