Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
First, two quick checks and a request for missing inputs, then concrete formulas, example calculations, and suggested finance narrative points you can drop into materials.

1) Quick inconsistency check
- You said: $5.2M cash, 14 months runway, burn $45K/month.
- math: 5,200,000 / 45,000 ≈ 115.6 months (≈ 9.6 years), not 14 months.
Please confirm which of these is correct (cash, runway, or burn). Often “runway = 14 months” implies an actual monthly net burn ~371k/month (5.2M / 14 ≈ 371k). Clarifying that will affect the fundraising story.

2) Inputs I need to compute CAC and LTV precisely
Please provide (if available):
- Period and scope for CAC: marketing + sales spend in the last quarter (or last 12 months) used for acquisition.
- Number of new customers acquired in that same period.
- Product model: subscription (SaaS) or one-time / repeat-purchase product.
- Average revenue per customer (ARPU) per month or average order value (AOV).
- Gross margin (contribution margin) per customer.
- Churn rate (monthly churn or annual churn) or average customer lifetime.
- Any segmentation (enterprise vs SMB) or channel splits (paid search, sales team, partners).

If you don’t have all inputs, I can show a sensitivity table so you can see ranges.

3) Core formulas (use these in slides/notes)
- CAC = (Total acquisition marketing + sales spend in period) / (Number of new customers acquired in that period)
- LTV (subscription, steady-state) = ARPU per period * Gross margin % / Churn rate per same period
  - e.g., monthly LTV = (monthly ARPU * gross_margin) / monthly_churn
- LTV (non-subscription / repeat buyers) = Average order value * purchases per period * gross_margin * expected lifetime (in periods)
- CAC payback period = CAC / (monthly ARPU * gross_margin)
- LTV : CAC ratio = LTV / CAC (rule of thumb: >3 is good; 1–3 needs scrutiny; <1 is unsustainable)
- Contribution per customer per month = monthly ARPU * gross_margin - variable costs attributed to the customer (if you track them)

4) Example calculations using your revenue number (illustrative)
Facts: revenue last quarter = $3.2M → monthly revenue ≈ $1.067M. I’ll show a few scenarios so you can see sensitivity. Assume gross margin = 70% (adjust if different).

Scenario A — lower ARPU / higher churn (SMB)
- Monthly ARPU = $200
- Monthly churn = 3% (0.03)
- Monthly LTV = 200 * 0.70 / 0.03 = $4,667
- If CAC = $1,500 → LTV/CAC = 3.11 (healthy); CAC payback = 1,500 / (200*0.7) ≈ 10.7 months

Scenario B — mid ARPU / moderate churn
- Monthly ARPU = $1,000
- Monthly churn = 2% (0.02)
- Monthly LTV = 1,000 * 0.70 / 0.02 = $35,000
- If CAC = $5,000 → LTV/CAC = 7; payback = 5,000 / (1,000*0.7) ≈ 7.1 months

Scenario C — enterprise / low churn
- Monthly ARPU = $10,000
- Monthly churn = 0.5% (0.005)
- Monthly LTV = 10,000 * 0.70 / 0.005 = $1,400,000
- If CAC = $150,000 → LTV/CAC ≈ 9.3; payback = 150,000 / (10,000*0.7) ≈ 21.4 months (long payback — common for large enterprise deals sold by field teams)

Takeaway from examples:
- LTV/CAC alone doesn’t tell the whole story; payback period matters for cash flow. High LTV/CAC with long payback can still strain cash if you need to front large CAC today.
- SaaS investors like LTV/CAC > 3 and payback < 12 months for aggressive growth; B2B enterprise often accepts longer payback.

5) Finance narrative structure (what to include in internal materials)
Use a 1–2 slide summary, then backup data/cohorts:
- Current status:
  - Cash: $5.2M (confirm)
  - Net burn: confirm monthly burn and whether it includes planned hiring/investments
  - Runway: recalc based on confirmed burn
  - ARR or annualized run rate from last quarter: $12.8M
- Unit economics headline:
  - CAC (by channel) and blended CAC
  - LTV (by segment) and blended LTV
  - LTV/CAC ratio and CAC payback period
- Cohort performance:
  - 3- and 12-month retention cohorts, gross margin contribution over time, CAC payback curve
- Sensitivity analysis:
  - Show 3 scenarios (base, downside, upside) for churn and ARPU and how that impacts LTV, payback, and cash runway (if you scale marketing spend)
- Ask / plan:
  - If raising: how much and why (growth spend vs runway extension), and expected impact (target CAC, improved retention, resulting ARR)
  - If not raising: milestones to extend runway (reduce burn, improve CAC or retention), hiring freezes, channel reallocation
- Risks and mitigations:
  - E.g., CAC creep in paid channels → shift to inbound/land-and-expand; churn spikes → product retention initiatives

6) Suggested KPIs to present
- ARR / QoQ growth %
- New customers (period)
- CAC by channel and blended CAC
- ARPU by segment
- Gross margin %
- Churn (net & gross) and retention curves
- LTV, LTV/CAC, CAC payback
- Months of cash runway (base case and after hiring/marketing plan)

7) Next steps I can do for you (pick any)
- If you give: total acquisition spend and number of new customers in the quarter (or last 12 months), gross margin, churn or ARPU — I’ll compute your CAC, LTV, LTV/CAC, and payback and produce a short bullet-ready narrative.
- Or, I can produce a sensitivity table using ranges you approve (ARPU, churn, CAC) and a one-page slide text you can paste into investor/internal deck.
- Or, I can draft the exact wording for the finance narrative slide (~6–8 bullets) once we confirm the runway/burn numbers.

Which would you like me to do next? If you want me to run numbers now, please share: acquisition spend, new customers, gross margin, and churn or ARPU (or tell me you want me to assume reasonable defaults).

