I can help — I’ll outline the right formulas, give concrete examples and guidance for the finance narrative, and show what inputs I need to compute CAC and LTV precisely for Stellar Labs.

Quick flag before we start
- The numbers you gave contain a possible inconsistency: cash = $5.2M and runway = 14 months implies net burn ≈ $371K/month (5.2M / 14). But you also listed burn rate = $45K/month. If burn is truly $45K/month, cash would cover ~115 months. Which figure is the operative “net burn / runway” you want to use for planning? I’ll proceed with general guidance but please confirm which is correct.

Key definitions and formulas (use these in your slides)
- CAC (Customer Acquisition Cost) = (Total Sales + Marketing spend in period) / (Number of New Customers acquired in same period)
  - Include: marketing spend, sales costs & commissions, agency fees, creative, ad spend, onboarding sales engineering time (if you capitalize for CAC).
  - You can report both blended CAC and channel-level CAC (paid ads, direct sales, partners).
- ARPA / ACV:
  - ARPA (average revenue per account) monthly = Monthly Revenue / Total Active Customers
  - ACV (average contract value) = Annualized ARPA per customer
- Gross contribution margin (GCM) = Revenue × (1 - COGS %)
  - Use contribution margin (after cost of goods sold) for LTV, not top-line revenue.
- Churn:
  - Period churn rate (monthly or annual) = customers lost in period / customers at start of period
  - Average customer lifetime (periods) = 1 / churn_rate (if churn is constant)
- LTV (simple, non-discounted) = ARPA_period × GCM × (1 / churn_rate_period)
  - If using annual churn: LTV = ACV × GCM × (1 / annual_churn)
