Quick note before we start: the numbers you gave contain a clear inconsistency we should resolve.

- You said cash = $5.2M and runway = 14 months. That implies a monthly burn of 5,200,000 / 14 = $371,429/month.
- But you also wrote burn = $45K/month. If burn were $45K/month, runway would be 5,200,000 / 45,000 = 115.6 months (~9.6 years).
Please confirm which burn/runway numbers are correct. I’ll proceed with CAC / LTV guidance while we clarify that.

What I need from you to compute CAC and LTV precisely
- Sales & Marketing spend (periodic): e.g., last quarter S&M spend (or last 12 months). Include paid acquisition, agency fees, sales commissions, related headcount.
- Number of customers acquired in the same period (new customers closed).
- Active customers (total paying customers) or ARPA/ARPU (average revenue per account / user) — specify monthly or annual.
- Gross margin on revenue (revenue less direct cost of goods sold) — as a percentage.
- Churn rate (monthly or annual) or average customer lifetime (in months or years).

Key formulas (and how to use them)
- CAC = Total Sales & Marketing spend for a period / Number of new customers acquired in that period.
  - Example: $300,000 S&M last quarter / 150 new customers = $2,000 CAC.
- LTV (common simple formula) = ARPU (per period) × Gross margin % × Average customer lifetime (in same periods).
  - If you use churn: Average lifetime (months) = 1 / monthly churn rate.
  - Example (monthly basis): ARPU = $1,000/mo, gross margin = 70%, monthly churn = 2% → lifetime = 1/0.02 = 50 months → LTV = $1,000 × 0.70 × 50 = $35,000.
- LTV : CAC ratio = LTV / CAC — rule-of-thumb targets:
  - > 3 : 1 is healthy
  - 1 : 1 or below is unsustainable
- Payback period on CAC = CAC / (contribution margin per month)
  - Contribution margin per month = ARPU × gross margin.
  - Target: payback < 12 months (preferably < 6–12 months for high-growth companies).

Illustrative scenarios using your revenue figure (3.2M last quarter)
I’ll give 3 brief scenarios so you can see how assumptions change LTV/CAC. All use the same quarterly revenue but different customer counts and churn assumptions.

Scenario A — Enterprise-ish customers (low count, high ARPU)
- Revenue last quarter = $3.2M → monthly ≈ $1.067M.
- Assume active customers = 100 → ARPU ≈ $10,667/mo.
- Gross margin = 70%, monthly churn = 1% → lifetime = 100 months.
- Contribution margin/mo = $10,667 × 0.7 = $7,467.
- LTV = $7,467 × 100 = $746,700.
- If CAC = $50,000 (enterprise sales), LTV:CAC ≈ 14.9 (excellent). Payback ≈ 6.7 months.

Scenario B — Mid-market (moderate count)
- Active customers = 500 → ARPU ≈ $2,133/mo.
- Gross margin = 70%, monthly churn = 2% → lifetime = 50 months.
- Contribution margin/mo = $1,493.
- LTV = $1,493 × 50 = $74,650.
- If CAC = $6,000, LTV:CAC ≈ 12.4. Payback ≈ 4 months.

Scenario C — SMB/high-volume (high count, lower ARPU)
- Active customers = 2,000 → ARPU ≈ $533/mo.
- Gross margin = 70%, monthly churn = 5% → lifetime = 20 months.
- Contribution margin/mo = $373.
- LTV = $373 × 20 = $7,460.
- If CAC = $1,200, LTV:CAC ≈ 6.2. Payback ≈ 3.2 months.

These examples show how sensitive LTV and LTV:CAC are to ARPU and churn. Use your real active customer count, churn, and S&M spend to get accurate numbers.

