Which of those is correct (burn = $45k, or runway = 14 months)? Or is the 14‑month runway a projection that assumes some future hiring/one‑time spend? I’ll proceed by (A) giving the exact formulas, (B) listing the inputs I need to compute CAC and LTV precisely, (C) showing two short example scenarios so you can see how the numbers shift, and (D) giving recommended finance narrative language and KPIs to include.

1) What I need from you to compute accurate CAC and LTV
- Time period you want to analyze (last quarter, last 12 months, trailing 12 months)  
- Total Sales + Marketing spend over that period (S&M)  
- Number of new customers acquired in that same period (new paid customers)  
- Total customers (for ARPU or ACV calculations) or cohort counts if available  
- Average Revenue Per User (ARPU) or Average Contract Value (ACV) (monthly or annual)  
- Billing cadence (monthly, annual) and gross margin % on revenue (or cost of goods sold)  
- Churn rate (monthly or annual) or average customer lifetime (if known)  
- Any big non-recurring S&M spends or sales commissions you want excluded/treated separately

If you give me those, I’ll calculate CAC, LTV, LTV:CAC, CAC payback and produce a short finance-ready paragraph and a one-slide summary.

2) Definitions and formulas (simple, standard)
- CAC = (Total Sales + Marketing spend in period) / (Number of new customers acquired in that period)  
- LTV (simple cohort model) = ARPU_period * Gross Margin% / churn_rate_period  
  - If using monthly units: LTV = ARPU_monthly * GrossMargin% / monthly_churn  
  - If using annual units: LTV = ARPU_annual * GrossMargin% / annual_churn  
- Alternate LTV = ARPU * GrossMargin% * (1 / churn) = contribution margin per period × expected number of periods  
- CAC payback (months) = CAC / (ARPU_monthly * Gross Margin%)  (if contribution margin flows monthly)  
- LTV:CAC ratio = LTV / CAC  (benchmark: >3 healthy; 1–3 needs improvement; <1 unsustainable)

3) Two short example scenarios using your revenue as context
Assume revenue last quarter $3.2M (≈ $1.067M/mo). I’ll show a B2B and a Self‑serve example:

Example A — Enterprise / high-ACV model
- Suppose last quarter S&M = $600k and new customers = 12 → CAC = $600k / 12 = $50k
- Suppose ACV per new customer ≈ $200k (so monthly ARPU ≈ $16.7k), gross margin = 80%, annual churn = 10% (monthly churn ≈ 0.83% ≈ 0.0083)
  - LTV (annual churn formula): LTV = ACV * GrossMargin / annual_churn = $200k * 0.8 / 0.10 = $1.6M
  - LTV:CAC = $1.6M / $50k = 32 → extremely favorable (typical for enterprise if retention is strong)
  - CAC payback (months) = CAC / (monthly contribution) = $50k / (16.7k * 0.8) ≈ $50k / $13.3k ≈ 3.8 months

Example B — Self‑serve / volume model
- Suppose last quarter S&M = $600k and new customers = 3,000 → CAC = $600k / 3,000 = $200
- Suppose ARPU = $40/month, gross margin = 75%, monthly churn = 3% (0.03)
  - LTV = ARPU_monthly * GM / monthly_churn = $40 * 0.75 / 0.03 = $1,000
  - LTV:CAC = $1,000 / $200 = 5 → healthy
  - CAC payback = $200 / ($40 * 0.75) = $200 / $30 ≈ 6.7 months

Key takeaway from examples: LTV is highly sensitive to churn and gross margin; CAC depends on how many customers you acquire for the same spend.

4) Benchmarks (use in narrative)
- LTV:CAC > 3 is a good rule-of-thumb for SaaS/recurring models. Above 3 indicates scalable economics; 1–3 means fix retention or reduce CAC; <1 is unsustainable.  
- CAC payback < 12 months is solid for growth-stage companies (shorter is better if you prefer capital efficiency).  
- Gross margin matters: lower gross margin reduces LTV materially; customer retention (churn) typically has the biggest impact on LTV.

