I can definitely help — I’ll show the formulas, what inputs we need, run a few realistic scenarios from assumptions (so you can see the sensitivity), and give a concise finance narrative you can drop into internal materials. First, I need a couple of missing numbers to compute exact CAC and LTV for Stellar Labs:

Please provide (if available):
- Sales & Marketing spend for the period you want to measure (quarterly or annual). If you don’t have this, tell me S&M as a % of revenue or I’ll use a benchmark.
- Number of new customers acquired in that same period (or total active customers so we can infer ARPU).
- Gross margin (or cost of goods sold as a %). If unknown, I can use a benchmark (70–85% for many SaaS/tech businesses).
- Customer churn rate (annual churn %) or average customer lifetime (months/years). If not known, I’ll show a range of churn scenarios.

What I can do immediately
- Show the formulas and explain each metric
- Run 3 illustrative scenarios (low ARPU / high ARPU / mid) using your $3.2M last quarter (annualized to $12.8M ARR) so you can see how CAC and LTV change with ARPU, churn and S&M spend
- Give the finance narrative and recommendations (target LTV:CAC, payback period thresholds, how to talk about runway and marketing investments)

Formulas (short)
- CAC = Total Sales & Marketing spend in a period / Number of new customers acquired in that period
- ARPA (average revenue per account, annual) = ARR / number_of_customers
- LTV = ARPA * Gross Margin (%) / Annual Churn Rate
  - Equivalent: LTV = Monthly ARPU * Gross Margin / Monthly Churn
- LTV : CAC ratio = LTV / CAC
- Payback period (months) = CAC / (Monthly ARPA * Gross Margin)

Benchmarks to keep in mind
- Healthy LTV:CAC: >= 3x (often target 3–5x depending on stage)
- Payback period: <12 months is aggressive/growth-stage; 12–18 months acceptable; >24 months may be problematic unless CAC is strategic and LTV is huge
- Gross margin: software/SaaS often 70–90%; hardware/lower-margin products will reduce LTV materially

Illustrative scenarios (using ARR = 12.8M from $3.2M last quarter)
Assumptions that vary: number of customers (so ARPA changes), S&M spend (as % of revenue), churn, gross margin.

Scenario A — Many small customers (low ARPU)
- Customers = 1,280 → ARPA = $10,000/year (monthly ARPU ≈ $833)
- Assume S&M = 30% of revenue → S&M = $3.84M/year
- Suppose new customers acquired = 200/year → CAC = $3.84M / 200 = $19,200
- Gross margin = 70%, annual churn = 20% → LTV = 10,000 * 0.7 / 0.20 = $35,000
- LTV:CAC = 35k / 19.2k ≈ 1.8x (below target)
- Payback = 19,200 / (833 * 0.7) ≈ 33 months (long)

Implication: With low ARPU and high churn, you need either lower CAC, higher retention, or higher pricing/expansion to reach healthy unit economics.

Scenario B — Mid-market customers
- Customers = 320 → ARPA = $40,000/year (monthly ≈ $3,333)
- S&M = 30% revenue → $3.84M/year
- New customers = 80/year → CAC = 3.84M / 80 = $48,000
- Gross margin = 80%, churn = 10% → LTV = 40,000 * 0.8 / 0.10 = $320,000
- LTV:CAC ≈ 6.7x (very healthy)
- Payback = 48,000 / (3,333 * 0.8) ≈ 14.4 months

Implication: Higher ARPA and lower churn yields very strong unit economics even with higher CAC.

Scenario C — Enterprise / high-ticket
- Customers = 128 → ARPA = $100,000/year
- S&M = 30% revenue → $3.84M/year
- New customers = 20/year → CAC = 3.84M / 20 = $192,000
- Gross margin = 85%, churn = 8% → LTV = 100,000 * 0.85 / 0.08 = $1,062,500
- LTV:CAC ≈ 5.5x
- Payback = 192,000 / ((100,000/12) * 0.85) ≈ 22.7 months

Implication: Enterprise sales tolerate higher CAC and longer payback if LTV is very large and churn is low—but board may want to see projected cash flow given longer payback.

How this ties to your runway and finance narrative
- You have $5.2M cash and 14 months runway (you stated this). Before proposing S&M increases, show scenario analyses that map incremental S&M spend → customer growth → change in burn and expected ARR uplift over 12–24 months.
- If your current burn is only $45K/month, you have room to temporarily increase S&M to accelerate growth, but show sensitivity: e.g., doubling S&M will increase burn short-term; show required CAC payback to avoid depleting cash too quickly.
- Core finance narrative bullets:
  - “We will target an LTV:CAC of >= 3x (strewn toward 4x for sustained profitability); primary levers are reducing churn and improving ARPU through upsells/packaging.”
  - “Prioritize retention initiatives (X% expected churn reduction) before large S&M scale-up — this improves ROI on acquisition spend.”
  - “We recommend a staged S&M ramp tied to payback thresholds (only scale if payback < 12–18 months and projected LTV:CAC ≥ 3x).”
  - “We will run monthly unit-economics reporting (CAC by acquisition channel, cohort LTV, payback months) to inform budget reallocation.”
  - “With $5.2M cash and current runway, we can fund a disciplined S&M ramp if modelled scenarios show payback < 18 months or have clear ARR uplift milestones.”

Next steps I can run for you (pick one)
