Quick note before calculations: there’s an inconsistency in the numbers you gave that I need you to confirm.

- You said cash = $5.2M and runway = 14 months. That implies monthly net burn ≈ $5.2M / 14 ≈ $371k/month.
- You also said burn rate = $45k/month. If burn truly is $45k/month, runway would be ~115 months (5.2M / 45k).

Please confirm which monthly burn is correct (or whether $45k is net burn after some adjustments), because runway and cash position matter for the narrative and recommended actions.

What I can do right now
- Give you the exact CAC and LTV formulas and the inputs I need.
- Run a few realistic scenario examples using your revenue number (Q revenue $3.2M → annualized revenue ≈ $12.8M) so you can see how unit economics change with assumptions.
- Give a short finance narrative you can drop into slides or a CFO memo and recommended targets/actions.

Key formulas and definitions
- CAC = (Total Sales & Marketing spend over period) / (Number of new customers acquired in that period)
- ARPA (annual) = Annual revenue / Number of active customers
- Customer lifetime (years) = 1 / annual churn rate
- LTV (gross-margin-based, annual terms) = ARPA * Gross Margin % * (1 / annual churn rate)
- LTV : CAC ratio = LTV / CAC
- Payback period (months) = CAC / (ARPA * Gross Margin % / 12)

Required inputs (please supply these for exact numbers)
- Total S&M spend (same period as new customers) — typically annual S&M
- Number of new customers in that period (or total customers and growth)
- Total active customers (to compute ARPA), or average contract value (ACV)
- Gross margin (percent)
- Churn rate (annual or monthly; if monthly, I’ll convert)
If you don’t have these, I’ll run scenario examples.

Scenario examples (using your revenue: annual = $12.8M)
I’ll show three archetypes (Enterprise, Mid-market, SMB) with plausible numbers so you can see sensitivity.

Assumptions common to all scenarios unless stated:
- Annual revenue = $12.8M
- S&M spend = 25% of revenue = $3.2M/year (changeable)
- Gross margin = 75% (adjust if different)

1) Enterprise-style (high ACV, lower number of customers)
- Active customers: 200 → ARPA = $12.8M / 200 = $64,000/year
- New customers/year: 200 (growth = replacement + net adds)
- CAC = $3.2M / 200 = $16,000
- Assume annual churn = 10% → customer lifetime = 10 years
- LTV = 64,000 * 0.75 / 0.10 = $480,000
- LTV:CAC = 480k / 16k = 30x
- Payback months = 16k / (64k * 0.75 / 12) = 4 months

Takeaway: very attractive unit economics — long LTV, fast payback.

2) Mid-market
- Active customers: 800 → ARPA = $12.8M / 800 = $16,000/year
- New customers/year: 300
- CAC = $3.2M / 300 = $10,667
- Annual churn = 10% → LTV = 16,000 * 0.75 / 0.10 = $120,000
- LTV:CAC ≈ 11.3x
- Payback ≈ 10.7 months

Takeaway: strong unit economics; payback near the commonly-accepted 12-month target.

3) SMB / volume-driven
- Active customers: 3,200 → ARPA = $12.8M / 3,200 = $4,000/year
- New customers/year: 1,000
- CAC = $3.2M / 1,000 = $3,200
- Annual churn = 20% → LTV = 4,000 * 0.75 / 0.20 = $15,000
- LTV:CAC ≈ 4.7x
- Payback ≈ 12.8 months

Takeaway: still positive, but payback and churn are tighter — more sensitive to churn and S&M efficiency.

Benchmarks & targets (guidance)
- LTV:CAC: aim for >= 3x; 4–5x is excellent if sustainable.
- Payback period: <12 months is desirable for fast growth; up to 18 months can be OK for later-stage companies.
- Gross margin: SaaS/recurring software generally targets 70–80%+.
- Churn: lower is better. Annual churn <10% is strong for B2B; SMBs often experience higher churn.

Suggested finance narrative (short — use on a slide or in notes)
- “Unit economics are healthy: with annualized revenue of $12.8M, we target S&M spend of ~25% of revenue ($3.2M/year). Under our mid-market model (ARPA ~$16k, 300 new customers/year, 10% churn), CAC ~ $10.7k and LTV ~ $120k, producing an LTV:CAC of ~11x and ~11-month payback. These metrics support continued sales investment while preserving runway.”
- “Key sensitivities: LTV is highly sensitive to churn and gross margin; CAC is sensitive to sales efficiency and channel mix. A 5 pp increase in churn or a 20% increase in CAC materially lengthens payback and reduces LTV:CAC.”
- “Actions to preserve and extend runway: prioritize retention initiatives (reduce churn), focus marketing on the highest-converting channels to lower CAC, and push upsell/expansion to increase ARPA and LTV.”

Concrete next steps I can do for you
