I can help — but I need a few missing inputs to compute CAC and LTV precisely. First, two quick notes from the numbers you gave:

- You said cash = $5.2M, burn = $45K/month and runway = 14 months. Those three don’t line up: 5.2M / 45K ≈ 115 months of runway. If runway is 14 months, either the runway calculation includes planned increases in spend (e.g., hiring, S&M ramp) or burn will rise materially. Can you confirm which is correct?
- Revenue last quarter = $3.2M → monthly revenue ≈ $1,066,667 and annualized ≈ $12.8M. That’s helpful for per-customer averages once we know customer counts.

What I need from you to calculate CAC and LTV accurately
- Sales & Marketing spend for the period you want to measure (monthly or quarterly or annual). CAC uses S&M spend over the same period as new customers acquired.
- Number of new customers acquired in that period (or deals/ACVs).
- Number of active customers (or average customers in period), or average contract value (ACV) or average revenue per account (ARPA).
- Gross margin (contribution margin) on revenue (typical SaaS: 70–80% — but we should use your actual number).
- Churn rate (monthly or annual) or average customer lifetime (in years or months). If you only have retention, that can be converted.

Formulas — quick reference
- CAC = (Total Sales & Marketing spend in period) / (New customers acquired in same period)
- ARPA_monthly = (Revenue in period) / (Average # of active customers in same period) [or ACV/12]
- LTV (simple, MRR basis) = ARPA_monthly * Gross Margin (%) / Monthly churn rate
  - Equivalent on annual basis: LTV = ACV * Gross Margin / Annual churn
- LTV:CAC ratio = LTV / CAC
- CAC payback (months) = CAC / (ARPA_monthly * Gross Margin)

Benchmarks & targets to use in the narrative
- Healthy LTV:CAC target: ≥ 3x (some high-growth companies accept lower in early ramp if payback <12 months)
- CAC payback target: < 12 months (often <18 months during heavy growth)
- Gross margins for software businesses: typically 70–80% (lower for hardware or heavy services)
- Use cohort analysis (LTV by cohort, CAC by channel) — investors want channel-level CAC and CAC payback over time.

Example scenarios (illustrative — replace with your real inputs)
Assumptions derived from your revenue (quarter = $3.2M → monthly ≈ $1,066,667):
Scenario A — mid-market SaaS
- Annual revenue = $12.8M
- Assume ACV = $10,000 → average customers ≈ 1,280
- S&M spend = 30% of revenue = $3.84M/year
- New customers in year = 500 → CAC = 3,840,000 / 500 = $7,680
- ARPA_monthly = ACV/12 = $833
- Gross margin = 75%, annual churn = 10% → monthly churn ≈ 0.88% (approx)
- LTV = (833 * 0.75) / 0.0088 ≈ $70,900
- LTV:CAC ≈ 70,900 / 7,680 ≈ 9.2x — very strong
- CAC payback months = 7,680 / (833 * 0.75) ≈ 12.3 months

Scenario B — enterprise / higher ACV
- ACV = $50,000 → customers ≈ 256
- S&M = 20% revenue = $2.56M/year
- New customers/year = 50 → CAC = 2,560,000 / 50 = $51,200
- ARPA_monthly = 50,000/12 = $4,167
- Gross margin = 80%, annual churn = 8% → monthly churn ≈ 0.67%
- LTV = (4,167 * 0.8) / 0.0067 ≈ $497,000
- LTV:CAC ≈ 497,000 / 51,200 ≈ 9.7x
- CAC payback months = 51,200 / (4,167 * 0.8) ≈ 15.4 months

How to present this in your internal materials (concise narrative + recommended slides)
