I can help — but I’ll need a few inputs to produce accurate CAC and LTV and to craft the finance narrative. Below I’ll (1) point out an inconsistency in the numbers you gave, (2) give the exact formulas and KPIs you should report, (3) show example calculations/scenarios so you can see how the math works, and (4) list what I need from you to produce precise CAC/LTV numbers and a ready-to-use finance narrative or slide text.

1) Quick checks / inconsistency
- You said: cash = $5.2M and runway = 14 months. That implies an implied net cash burn of ~ $5.2M / 14 ≈ $371k/month.
- You also said burn rate = $45k/month. Those two facts conflict. Which is correct for “net cash burn” (cash outflows minus cash inflows)? If net burn really is $45k/mo, runway = $5.2M / $45k ≈ 115 months, not 14.
- Revenue last quarter = $3.2M → average monthly revenue last quarter ≈ $1.067M; annualizing that quarter gives ~ $12.8M ARR (if revenue is stable).

Please confirm which burn/runway numbers are accurate (or explain what “burn” refers to in your report).

2) Key formulas and metrics to compute and present
- CAC (Customer Acquisition Cost) = Total Sales & Marketing spend in period / Number of new customers acquired in same period.
- ARPU (Average Revenue Per User / Customer) = Revenue in period / Active customers in period (choose monthly or annual basis consistently).
- Gross margin % = (Revenue − Cost of goods sold) / Revenue. Use contribution margin if applicable (exclude S&M and G&A).
- Monthly churn rate = Customers lost in month / Customers at start of month.
- Customer lifetime (months) ≈ 1 / monthly churn rate (if churn is roughly constant).
- LTV (simple) = ARPU_per_month * Gross margin % * Customer lifetime_months.
  - Alternatively LTV = ARPU_annual * Gross margin % * Average customer lifetime in years.
- LTV : CAC ratio = LTV / CAC (healthy rule of thumb: ~3x or higher).
- CAC payback (months) = CAC / (ARPU_per_month * Gross margin %). (Target often <12 months for aggressive growth-stage companies.)

3) Example scenarios (to show how results change with assumptions)
I’ll show two illustrative examples so you can see the sensitivity:

Example A — Enterprise / high ARPU
- Assumptions: S&M spend (quarter) = $900k → $300k/month; new customers in quarter = 30 → avg 10 new customers/month.
  - CAC = $900k / 30 = $30k per customer.
- ARPU = $5k/month per customer; gross margin = 75%; monthly gross contribution per customer = $5k * 0.75 = $3.75k.
- Suppose monthly churn = 1% → lifetime ≈ 1 / 0.01 = 100 months (~8.3 years).
  - LTV = $3.75k * 100 ≈ $375k.
  - LTV:CAC = $375k / $30k = 12.5x (very healthy).
  - Payback = $30k / $3.75k ≈ 8 months.

Example B — SMB / lower ARPU
- Assumptions: S&M spend (quarter) = $450k; new customers = 300 → CAC = $1.5k.
- ARPU = $500/month; gross margin = 60% → monthly gross contribution = $300.
- Monthly churn = 5% → lifetime ≈ 20 months.
  - LTV = $300 * 20 = $6k.
  - LTV:CAC = $6k / $1.5k = 4x (healthy).
  - Payback = $1.5k / $300 = 5 months.

These show why you must present assumptions (ARPU, churn, gross margin) and granular CAC by channel/cohort.

4) What I need from you to calculate CAC and LTV for Stellar Labs
Please provide (ideally for the last quarter and last 12 months):
- Total Sales & Marketing spend (quarterly and annual).
- Number of new customers acquired in that period (and whether they’re enterprise/mid-market/SMB).
- Total number of active customers (to compute ARPU if you don’t have ARPU directly).
